1.  *The point and the selected places satisfy $$\begin{aligned}
     h_{\mathrm{aff}}(z)&\leq C_0d^{C_0}h,
     &|a|_v&<1\quad(v\in V),\label{int:global-input}\\
     \sum_{v\in V}w_v\log |a|_v^{-1}
       &\geq \frac{h}{C_0d^{C_0}},
     &\sum_{v\in V}w_v\log^+\|U\|_v
       &\leq C_0d^{C_0}(1+\log h)^{C_0}.
     \label{int:local-input}
    \end{aligned}$$ Here $\|U\|_v$ is the maximum of the coordinate absolute values.

outputs.exact_statement:
In proving [intro:squares](#intro:squares), we may work with a smooth geometrically irreducible curve $S$ over a fixed number field, a principally polarized abelian scheme $\mathscr A\to S$, and a nonconstant finite map from $S$ onto a dense open of the original curve $C$. We may equip the family with full symplectic levels at two distinct fixed primes at least three. Under the supposition that the required elliptic-square locus is infinite, we may fix a point $t_0\in S(\overline{\mathbb Q})$ in that locus.*


*Further fixed finite covers, normalization, and removal of finite sets preserve this reduction. After enlarging a fixed field of definition $K$, there is a constant $b$ such that every selected lift $t$ of a retained coarse point $s$ satisfies $$\label{geo:degree-descent}
 [K(s):K]\leq [K(t):K]\leq b[K(s):K].$$ The smooth projective completion of $S$ and the divisor $[t_0]$ are available even when $C$ is complete.

The system $\mathcal P$ has rank five and a split nondegenerate trace pairing. Conjugation induces a surjective morphism $$\mathop{\mathrm{GSp}}_4\longrightarrow\mathop{\mathrm{SO}}(\mathcal P)$$ with scalar kernel. Its restriction to $\mathop{\mathrm{Sp}}_4$ is the spin covering, with kernel $\{1,-1\}$, and $\mathcal P$ is absolutely irreducible. For every odd prime $\ell$, the lattice $$\label{geo:integral-lattice}
 \Lambda_\ell=
 \{D\in\mathop{\mathrm{End}}_{\mathbb Z_\ell}(T_\ell\mathscr A_t):
       D^\dagger=D,\ \mathop{\mathrm{Tr}}(D)=0\}$$ is free of rank five with unimodular trace pairing.*

*If $\mathscr A_t$ is isogenous to the square of a non-CM elliptic curve, then $$\label{geo:hodge-plane}
 F_t=\mathop{\mathrm{End}}^0(\mathscr A_t)\cap\mathcal P_t$$ is a rational two-dimensional plane on which the trace pairing is positive definite. Here the intersection denotes Hodge endomorphisms in the Betti realization, equivalently actual rational endomorphisms in every realization.

After one fixed finite cover of the curve in [geo:setup](#geo:setup), there are distinct fixed odd primes $\ell_1,\ell_2$ and a fixed lift of the center $t_0$ with the following properties. Every non-CM elliptic-square target on the previous curve has a lift $t$ for which, in the respective marked five-spaces, $$\begin{aligned}
 \overline\Pi_{t_0,\ell_1}+\overline\Pi_{t,\ell_1}
   &\text{ is a degenerate four-space},\label{inc:first}\\
 \overline\Pi_{t_0,\ell_2}+\overline\Pi_{t,\ell_2}
   &\text{ is a nondegenerate four-space}.\label{inc:second}
\end{aligned}$$ The prime $\ell_1$ divides none of the fixed Hecke multipliers in [geo:hecke-list](#geo:hecke-list). The prime $\ell_2$ is different from every residue characteristic at which the fixed center is not potentially good. Both center-plane reductions are nondegenerate. The conditions remain valid upon simultaneous conjugation of all the data, and the degree of any chosen lift increases by at most a fixed factor.

Let $t$ be a target with $\mathop{\mathrm{End}}_{\overline{\mathbb Q}}(\mathcal A_t)\otimes\mathbb Q\simeq\mathop{\mathrm{Mat}}_2(\mathbb Q)$, and let $\mathcal O_t=\mathop{\mathrm{End}}_{\overline{\mathbb Q}}(\mathcal A_t)$. For the principal Rosati involution $\dagger$, set $$Q_t(f)=\mathop{\mathrm{Tr}}_{H_1}(ff^\dagger).$$ Then $$\label{ht:small-bounds}
 h_F(\mathcal A_t)\leq Ch,\qquad
 |\mathop{\mathrm{disc}}\mathcal O_t|\leq C(dh)^\kappa.$$ The discriminant here is that of the full geometric order for the trace of the regular representation of $\mathop{\mathrm{Mat}}_2(\mathbb Q)$. There are integral endomorphisms $f_1,\ldots,f_4$ spanning $\mathcal O_t\otimes\mathbb Q$ and an independent labeled pair $\mathbf P=(P_1,P_2)$ spanning its Rosati-symmetric trace-free plane, all with $$\label{ht:rosati-small}
 Q_t(f_i),\ Q_t(P_j)\leq C(dh)^\kappa.$$ All geometric endomorphisms, and hence this pair and its de Rham coordinates, are defined over an extension $L/K(t)$ of bounded degree. The bound depends only on the dimension and the fixed data.

The labeled pair $\mathbf P$ of [ht:endomorphisms](#ht:endomorphisms), expressed in the fixed rational $\mathcal P_{\rm dR}$ frame, satisfies $$\label{ht:global-coordinates}
 h_{\rm aff}(\mathbf P)\leq C(h+\log d).$$ Let $V$ be any subcollection of sufficiently close places in the actual center branches, with bad-center places excluded as in [inc:bad-disks](#inc:bad-disks). Then $$\label{ht:near-coordinates}
 \sum_{v\in V}n_v\log\max\{1,\|P_1\|_{v,\mathrm{coord}},
                                  \|P_2\|_{v,\mathrm{coord}}\}
       \leq C(1+\log h+\log d).$$ Both estimates hold for simultaneously conjugated data. They also hold for a fixed number of endomorphisms $F_j$ with $NF_j\in\mathcal O_t$ for a fixed positive integer $N$ and $Q_t(F_j)\leq C(dh)^\kappa$. If $\phi:\mathcal A_t\to\mathcal A_{t'}$ is a polarized isogeny of one of the fixed multipliers in [geo:hecke-list](#geo:hecke-list), write $I_t=\mathop{\mathrm{Ad}}(\phi)$, so $I_t(D)=\phi D\phi^{-1}$. In particular the estimates hold for the transported pair $\widehat{\mathbf P}=I_t^{-1}\mathbf P'$. The added choices in this last assertion have bounded relative field degree.

For fixed constants $c>0$ and $C$, every selected target satisfies $$\label{loc:mass-estimate}
 \sum_{v\in V_0}w_v\log\frac1{|a|_v}
 \geq c h-C\bigl(1+\log(dh)\bigr)^2.$$ The constants are uniform over arbitrary ramification of the target fields. They may depend on the fixed disks and on $\mathcal S$.

Let $C\subset\mathcal A_2$ be a curve as in [intro:squares](#intro:squares). There are a number field $K$ defining $C$ and constants $c,\delta>0$ such that every $s\in\Sigma_{E^2}(C)$ satisfies $$\label{fin:orbit-bound}
 \#\bigl(\operatorname{Aut}(\mathbb C/K)\cdot s\bigr)
   =[K(s):K]\ \geq\ c\Delta_s^\delta.$$ The constants are uniform over the whole indicated locus.

Let $W$ be an irreducible affine variety of dimension $k\geq1$, and let $a$ be a nonconstant regular coordinate. Suppose a polynomial $F$ and an integer $m\geq1$ satisfy $$\mathop{\mathrm{ord}}_D F<m\mathop{\mathrm{ord}}_Da$$ on every normalized divisor above $W\cap\{a=0\}$. Let $W'$ be the affine closure of the graph $$q=g:=a^{-m}F\quad\text{over }W\setminus\{a=0\},$$ and let $\pi:W'\to W$ be the projection. Then $$\label{int:projected-boundary}
 \dim\overline{\pi(W'\cap\{a=0\})}\leq k-2.$$ In particular, for any polynomial $f(U)$, $$\label{int:graph-cut}
 \dim\bigl(W'\cap\{a=0, q+f(U)=0\}\bigr)\leq k-2.$$ When $k=1$ these sets are empty. Degrees and heights of $W'$ admit the bounds of [int:algebra](#int:algebra) in terms of defining equations of $W$, $F$, and $m$.

Fix $M$. In $\overline{\mathbb Q}[a,U,q]$, with $M$ variables in total, let $I=(I_1,\ldots,I_r)$ be generated by polynomials of controlled number, degree, and height. Suppose its zero set $W'$ is irreducible of dimension $k\geq1$. Let $$f_0=q+e_m(U),\qquad J=(I,a,f_0),\qquad
 F_n=f_0+\sum_{j=1}^{n-1}a^j e_{m+j}(U),$$ where $\deg e_{m+j}\leq C_0(1+m+j)$ and $\dim V(J)\leq k-2$, interpreted as emptiness for $k=1$. Let $D\geq2$ bound $r$, $m$, the generator degrees, and $C_0$. There are constants $B,B'$, polynomial in $D$ for fixed $M$, such that the following holds. Whenever $n,L\geq1$ and $$\label{int:condition-count}
 B'n(1+L+Bn+B)^{k-2}<\binom{L+k}{k},$$ there is a polynomial $P$ with $\deg P\leq L$, nonzero on $W'$, and $$\label{int:actual-membership}
 P\in(I,a^n,F_n).$$ For $k=1$ the left side of <a href="#int:condition-count" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="int:condition-count">[int:condition-count]</a> is replaced by zero. Both $P$ and a certificate for <a href="#int:actual-membership" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="int:actual-membership">[int:actual-membership]</a> can have degree polynomial in $D+L+n$ and coefficient height polynomial in $D+L+n$ times one plus the coefficient heights of $I_1,\ldots,I_r,e_m,\ldots,e_{m+n-1}$.*

*In particular, for any prescribed positive integer $c$, one can take $n=cL$ with $L$ polynomial in $D+c$.
